.TH WHISRS 1 "2026-10-31" "whisrs 0.1.32" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whisrs \- Linux-first voice-to-text dictation CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whisrs
.I subcommand
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whisrs
is a thin CLI client that communicates with
.BR whisrsd (1)
over a Unix domain socket.
It sends commands (toggle, cancel, status) and prints the daemon's response.
.PP
The
.B setup
subcommand runs an interactive onboarding flow and does not require the daemon.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Interactive onboarding \(em select a transcription backend, enter an API key,
test the microphone, and write
.IR config.toml .
Does not require the daemon to be running.
.TP
.B config
Open an interactive menu to edit any section of
.IR config.toml
\(em backend, language, hotkeys, overlay, command-mode LLM, and more.
On save, writes the file and restarts the daemon via systemd. Unlike
.B setup
this does not touch udev rules, the systemd unit, or compositor keybindings.
.TP
.B toggle
Start or stop dictation. If idle, begins audio capture. If recording,
stops capture and sends audio for transcription. Text is typed at the
cursor in the previously focused window.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-language\fR \fICODE\fR
Override the transcription language for this session only \(em no config
edit or daemon restart.
.I CODE
is an ISO 639\-1 code (e.g.
.BR en ,
.BR pl ),
optionally with a region (e.g.
.BR en\-US ),
or
.B auto
for automatic detection. Applies to the
.B toggle
that starts recording; the override ends with the session. A plain
.B toggle
keeps using the configured
.IR general.language ,
so an existing hotkey is unaffected. Bind a separate key per language to
dictate in each without switching settings.
.RE
.TP
.B cancel
Cancel the current dictation and discard captured audio. Returns the
daemon to the idle state. Has no effect if the daemon is not recording.
.TP
.B log
Show recent transcription history. Nothing is recorded when
.B [general] history
is
.BR false .
.RS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Number of entries to show. Defaults to
.BR 20 .
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Clear all history instead of showing it.
.RE
.TP
.B command
Command mode: select text, speak an instruction, and the LLM rewrites it
in place. Requires a selection. To write text where there is none, configure
an
.B [[llm_commands]]
entry with a generic \(lqtreat the following text as a request and output
only what is asked\(rq instruction \(em that path never reads the selection.
See
.I https://github.com/y0sif/whisrs/blob/main/docs/configuration.md
for the recipe.
.TP
\fBllm\-command\fR \fINAME\fR
Toggle a named custom LLM command (see
.B [[llm_commands]]
in
.IR config.toml ):
dictate, the LLM applies the configured instruction, and the result is
typed at the cursor. Press again to stop recording, same as
.BR toggle .
.TP
\fBllm\-command\-set\fR \fINAME\fR
Reprogram a named LLM command from the current selection: highlight the
new instruction text, then run this \(em it is saved to
.I config.toml
and applied live. Pairs with an entry's
.BR set_hotkey .
.TP
\fBspeak\fR [\fB\-\-text\fR \fITEXT\fR]
Read the current text selection aloud via text-to-speech. A second
.B speak
or a
.B cancel
stops playback. Alias:
.BR read .
With
.BR \-\-text ,
speak
.I TEXT
instead of the selection
.RB ( "\-\-text \-"
reads it from standard input); a read-aloud in progress is stopped first.
.TP
\fBagent\fR [\fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-text\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-new\fR] [\fB\-\-reset\fR] [\fB\-\-stop\fR]
Talk to a coding agent configured in
.B [[agents]]
(Claude Code, OpenCode, Codex or GitHub Copilot CLI). Without
.IR NAME ,
list the agents with their harness, state
.RB ( idle ,
.BR working ,
or
.BR needs\-you )
and the session's id (Claude Code's background id, which
.B claude attach
takes; OpenCode's session id, which
.B opencode \-\-session
takes; Codex's thread id, which
.B codex resume
takes; Copilot's session id, which
.B copilot \-\-resume=
takes). With
.IR NAME ,
record a voice instruction for that agent (the same as its hotkey; run again
to stop) and send it to the agent's session; the reply is spoken through
.B [tts]
when the turn ends, and the next turn continues the same session. A run
while that agent's turn is still working is refused with a short spoken
notice; when that agent's own reply or notice is playing, it only stops it,
and another agent's reply keeps playing.
.B \-\-text
sends
.I TEXT
instead of recording
.RB ( "\-\-text \-"
reads standard input).
.B \-\-new
records a voice instruction that starts a fresh session instead (the same as
the agent's
.BR new_session_hotkey ;
run it again, or press either hotkey, to stop and send); with
.BR \-\-text ,
it sends
.I TEXT
to a fresh session.
.B \-\-reset
forgets the session and sends nothing; the next turn starts a new one. Both
are refused while a turn of that agent runs (one that waits for you is named
by what it waits for, since a plain run answers it).
.B \-\-stop
stops the running turn and keeps the session. With no turn running it stops
work the session does on its own, such as a background task it started, which
refuses new turns until it ends. whisrs only ever touches sessions it started.
.TP
.B status
Query the dictation state (for a recording, see
.BR "whisrs recording status" ).
Prints one of:
.BR idle ,
.BR recording ,
.BR transcribing ,
.BR synthesizing ,
or
.BR speaking .
.TP
.B restart
Restart the whisrs daemon. Uses
.B systemctl --user restart whisrs.service
on systemd systems and
.B rc-service --user whisrs restart
on OpenRC systems, when the corresponding user service is installed;
otherwise prints guidance for manual restart. Does not require the daemon
to be running:
.B restart
will start a stopped service.
.TP
.B recording
Record the microphone and the system audio (the default output's monitor) as
two separate tracks and turn them into a transcript, for a meeting or call, a
lecture, a video or language practice. The tracks are transcribed after
.B stop
into a Markdown transcript whose speaker labels are your name
.RB ( [recording]
.BR user_name )
and \(lqOthers\(rq, or, when
.B [recording] diarization
tells the voices on the system audio apart, \(lqSpeaker 1\(rq,
\(lqSpeaker 2\(rq and so on (a single voice keeps \(lqOthers\(rq), unless a
voice is named: by the people given with
.BR \-\-with ,
or, with
.B [recording] name_speakers
(off by default; it sends transcript excerpts to the
.B [llm]
endpoint), by the LLM from a quote of the transcript that shows the name.
With
.B [recording] summary
(off by default; it sends the whole transcript to the
.B [llm]
endpoint), each transcript also gets a summary from the LLM, and a recording
you gave no title gets one.
Each track is transcribed in its own language:
.B [recording] mic_language
and
.BR system_language ,
which default to
.B [recording] language
and then to
.BR "[general] language" .
A recording keeps the languages it started with.
While a recording is in progress,
.BR "whisrs toggle" ,
.B whisrs command
and
.B whisrs llm\-command
refuse to start, since the microphone is shared with the recording.
Each recording lives in its own folder under the recordings directory (see
.BR FILES ),
named by its id
.RI ( YYYY\-MM\-DD_HHMM ,
plus a slug of the title). Wherever an
.I ID
is taken, it may be a full id, a unique id prefix, or
.B last
for the newest recording. Requires an action:
.RS
.TP
\fBstart\fR [\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-title\fR \fITITLE\fR] [\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-mic\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-system\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-others\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-shared\-mic\fR[=\fIBOOL\fR]] [\fB\-\-num\-speakers\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-with\fR \fINAMES\fR]
Start a recording. The optional title goes into the transcript and the
folder name.
.B \-l
sets the language of both tracks for this recording (an ISO 639 code such as
.BR de ,
optionally with a region, or
.BR auto ),
and
.B \-\-mic\-language
and
.B \-\-system\-language
set one track's, over
.BR \-l .
.B \-\-others
names the system track in the transcript instead of \(lqOthers\(rq.
.B \-\-shared\-mic
says several people share your mic, so its voices are told apart too
(\fB\-\-shared\-mic=false\fR turns
.B [recording] shared_mic
off for this recording).
.B \-\-num\-speakers
tells a diarization sidecar
.RB ( "[recording] diarization_url" )
how many voices the system audio holds (your mic, when there is no system
audio), from 0 (let it decide) to 20.
.B \-\-with
says who the recording is with, names separated by commas (at most 20), to
name the voices on the system audio: one voice and one name, or the last
voice left once the others have names.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the recording and queue it for transcription.
.TP
\fBtoggle\fR [\fB\-t\fR \fITITLE\fR] [\fB\-l\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-mic\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-system\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-others\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-shared\-mic\fR[=\fIBOOL\fR]] [\fB\-\-num\-speakers\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-with\fR \fINAMES\fR]
Start a recording when none is in progress, otherwise stop the one that is.
Meant for a compositor hotkey. The options are those of
.B start
and apply when this starts a recording.
.TP
.B cancel
Stop the recording and delete it.
.TP
.B status
Print whether a recording is in progress or processing, and which recordings
are waiting for the network.
.TP
\fBlist\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
List recordings, newest first, with id, start time, duration, status and title.
.B \-\-json
prints a JSON array instead.
.TP
\fBshow\fR \fIID\fR [\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fBmd\fR|\fBjson\fR|\fBtxt\fR]
Print a transcript:
.B md
(the default) prints the Markdown file,
.B json
prints
.IR recording.json ,
and
.B txt
prints plain text.
.TP
\fBpath\fR \fIID\fR
Print the recording's folder.
.TP
\fBcopy\fR \fIID\fR
Copy the transcript to the clipboard.
.TP
\fBname\fR \fIID\fR \fISPEAKER\fR \fINAME\fR
Name a speaker of a transcribed recording.
.I SPEAKER
is its id (such as
.BR S2 ),
\(lqSpeaker 2\(rq, or the name it has now;
.I NAME
may be several words, and an empty one (\fB""\fR) clears the name you gave.
The transcript and
.I recording.json
are rewritten at once, nothing is transcribed again, and
.B retry
keeps the name, which works while a summary is being made; a summary
made before the name is marked as such. Refused while the recording is
queued or being transcribed.
.TP
\fBretry\fR \fIID\fR [\fB\-l\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-mic\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-system\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] [\fB\-\-shared\-mic\fR[=\fIBOOL\fR]] [\fB\-\-num\-speakers\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-with\fR \fINAMES\fR]
Transcribe the recording again, for example after a failure or in another
language. A language given here replaces the one the recording saved (as for
.BR start ,
.B \-l
covers both tracks); a track left out keeps its own.
.BR \-\-shared\-mic ,
.B \-\-num\-speakers
and
.B \-\-with
likewise replace the saved settings, and leaving them out keeps them
.RB ( "\-\-with \(dq\(dq"
clears the names). A recording whose
transcription was interrupted three times (by a crash each time) is marked
failed instead of being resumed at startup; retry resets that count and
queues it again. After a failure, chunks already transcribed with the same
backend and settings, language included, are reused; a recording that finished
keeps none, so its retry sends both tracks again. With
.B [recording] summary
on, a retry makes a new summary; with it off, the summary is kept and
marked as from an earlier transcript.
.TP
\fBsummarize\fR \fIID\fR
Summarize a transcribed recording with the
.B [llm]
now, whatever
.B [recording] summary
says, without transcribing it again. The summary goes before the transcript
in the Markdown file and into
.I recording.json
.RB ( summary ),
and a recording you gave no title gets one of 3 to 6 words (a title you gave
is never changed). The default rules (a TL;DR, sections by topic, decisions
and next steps only when there are any) can be replaced with
.BR "[recording] summary_instruction" .
A transcript of 50 words or fewer is not sent. The job waits behind any
recording being transcribed, and a notification says when it is done; if it
fails, the recording keeps the summary it had and notes why. A summary
interrupted by a shutdown is made at the next start. One job runs at a
time: a running summary delays the next transcription until it ends.
.RE
.IP
.BR list ,
.B show
and
.B path
read the recordings directory directly and work without the daemon.
A build without the
.B recording
feature says so and exits 1, whatever the action.
.TP
.B mcp
Serve your recordings to an AI agent: a read\-only MCP (Model Context
Protocol) server on standard input and output, for a client such as Claude
Code
.RB ( "claude mcp add whisrs \-\- whisrs mcp" ).
Its tools list recordings, give one recording's details and summary, page
through a transcript and search titles, summaries and transcripts. It reads
the recordings directory directly, works without the daemon, and never
changes a recording. The
.B mcp
feature needs the
.B recording
feature, and a build without
.B mcp
says so and exits 1.
.SH LLM OUTPUT
Applies to
.B command
and to every
.B [[llm_commands]]
entry \(em both type the model's reply at the cursor.
.PP
A reply wrapped entirely in a Markdown code fence is unwrapped before it is
typed; a fence is never what you wanted at the cursor. A reply that contains
several fenced blocks is prose about code, not a wrapper, and is left alone.
Stripping has no opt-out, so an entry whose instruction asks for a fenced
block cannot produce one.
.PP
A reply spanning several lines is typed normally \(em translating a paragraph
or drafting an email is exactly what these commands are for. The one exception
is a terminal: there a line break is an Enter that runs a command you have not
read, so a multi-line reply aimed at a terminal is refused rather than typed.
It is written to the history, where
.B whisrs log
recovers it; with
.B [general] history
set to
.B false
there is no copy and it is discarded.
Ask for a one\-liner to get an injected result. The refusal also
applies with
.B [input] paste
set, since bracketed paste is the foreground program's choice and cannot be
relied on; and its notification fires even with
.B [general] notify
turned off, because nothing was typed.
With
.B [input] clipboard_only
set, there is no refusal: whisrs types and pastes nothing, so the reply is
copied to the clipboard like any other result. Pasting it into a shell yourself
carries the same caveat about bracketed paste.
.PP
Terminal detection needs
.BR WindowTracker::get_focused_window_class ,
which Hyprland, Niri, Sway and X11 implement. On GNOME and KDE it reports
nothing, so a terminal there is treated as an ordinary target and multi-line
replies are typed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WHISRS_GROQ_API_KEY
Groq API key. Overrides the value in
.IR config.toml .
.TP
.B WHISRS_OPENAI_API_KEY
OpenAI API key. Overrides the value in
.IR config.toml .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Directory for the Unix socket. The socket is created at
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/whisrs.sock .
Falls back to
.IR /tmp/whisrs\-<uid>.sock .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory for configuration. Config file is read from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/whisrs/config.toml .
Defaults to
.IR ~/.config/whisrs/config.toml .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/whisrs/config.toml
Main configuration file. Created by
.BR "whisrs setup" .
Contains backend selection, API keys, language, audio settings, and hooks.
Permissions should be 0600 since it may contain API keys.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/whisrs.sock
Unix domain socket for CLI\-daemon IPC.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/whisrs/recovery/
Recovery directory for audio that failed transcription.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/whisrs/recordings/
Recordings and their transcripts, one folder per recording (defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share/whisrs/recordings/ ;
override with
.B [recording] dir
in
.IR config.toml ).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/whisrs/agents.json
The session of each
.B [[agents]]
entry (mode 0600; defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share/whisrs/agents.json ).
Written only when agents are configured.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (daemon not running, invalid state transition, or setup failure).
.SH EXAMPLES
Run interactive setup:
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs setup
.RE
.PP
Start dictation (bind this to a hotkey):
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs toggle
.RE
.PP
Start dictation in Polish for this session only:
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs toggle \-l pl
.RE
.PP
Cancel a dictation:
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs cancel
.RE
.PP
Check daemon state:
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs status
.RE
.PP
Send a typed turn to the agent named jarvis:
.PP
.RS
.B whisrs agent jarvis \-\-text "run the tests and tell me what failed"
.RE
